CX.update({
'bath-and-north-east-somerset':dict(lic=EN_LIC2,
 ctrl=('No short-let control area or Article 4 direction for holiday lets (searched 26 Oct 2026). The council\'s Article 4 directions cover things like turning offices into homes in Bath, demolishing walls, estate agents\' boards, houses in multiple occupation (HMOs) in Bath and some protected land, not holiday-let use. The national planning data register has no Article 4 data published for the council. A 2018 council scrutiny report on short-term lets said London\'s 90-night rule has no effect in Bath & North East Somerset, and that most short lets in Bath (3 or fewer rooms) stay in the C3 dwellinghouse use class, so are not a material change of use; larger ones can be, depending on the facts. Much of Bath is a World Heritage Site and conservation area, so check before altering a building.',[BAA4,BASTL,'https://www.planning.data.gov.uk/entity/?dataset=article-4-direction&organisation_entity=38']),
 levy=(EN_LEVY[0]+' Bath & North East Somerset is part of the West of England Combined Authority, whose Mayor will get this power. On 10 Sep 2026 the Combined Authority said people and businesses will be able to have their say in the coming months before plans are finalised. No rate or start date has been set.',EN_LEVY[1]+[WECA]),
 fees=('No short-let fees. Council tax: on 30 Nov 2023 Full Council unanimously resolved (minute 56) to increase council tax on all second homes by 100% from 1 Apr 2025, so a furnished home that is nobody\'s main home pays 200%. The council\'s page (checked 26 Oct 2026) says it still charges the premium and that it shows on your annual bill. '+EN_EXC+' The council adds that the 12-month sale or letting exception runs from the date the home was first advertised, can be used only once, and cannot apply if the home was first put on the market before 1 Apr 2024. Claim an exception online with the council\'s second home premium exception claim form; if the home is actually someone\'s main home, tell the council about a change of circumstances. The council may ask for evidence and a visiting officer may check. Empty and unfurnished homes: at the same meeting the council agreed to charge the empty homes premium after 1 year from 1 Apr 2024 (minute 55). Rates are 100% (1 to 5 years), 200% (5 to 10 years) and 300% (10 years or more), and they continue when the home changes owner. Empty-home exceptions include major repairs or structural work (Class M, up to 12 months).'+EN_BRT,[BA,BA2,BA4,BA3,EN_BR]),
 dates=EDATES('30 Nov 2023: Full Council approved the second homes premium')),
'new-forest':dict(lic=EN_LIC2,
 ctrl=(ENPL26(' Much of the district is in the New Forest National Park, where the National Park Authority handles planning.')[0]+' The national planning data register lists 10 Article 4 directions for New Forest District Council and 26 for the National Park Authority. They cover things like gates and fences, building works, temporary uses and (since 2021 and 2023, across the National Park) temporary campsites, not holiday lets in houses. The National Park Authority\'s draft Local Plan (Regulation 19, August 2026) proposes that all net new open-market homes in the Park carry a principal residence condition so they cannot be second homes. It is not yet adopted and would not apply to existing homes.',ENPL26('')[1]+['https://www.planning.data.gov.uk/entity/?dataset=article-4-direction&organisation_entity=229','https://www.planning.data.gov.uk/entity/?dataset=article-4-direction&organisation_entity=401',NFNP]),
 levy=EN_LEVY,
 fees=('No short-let fees. Council tax: Full Council agreed in principle on 12 Dec 2022 and, on 26 Feb 2024, approved the formal determination to charge a 100% premium on furnished homes that are nobody\'s permanent residence from 1 Apr 2025, so second homes pay 200%. The 2026/27 council tax base (Cabinet 3 Dec 2025) still includes it, for about 1,210 homes. Most of the extra money goes to Hampshire County Council (about 70%), with about 9% to the district council. '+EN_EXC+' A Dec 2024 review recommended no extra local exceptions, and the council\'s page says it has no discretion to reduce, delay or waive the premium beyond the listed exceptions. To claim the sale or letting exception, email counciltax@nfdc.gov.uk with the date first advertised, the agent and online listing link, evidence the price or rent is a fair market value, the energy performance certificate and any other steps you are taking. Empty and unfurnished homes: since 1 Apr 2025 the premium starts after 1 year (it was 2 years before): 100% after 1 year, 200% after 5 years and 300% after 10 years. Empty-home exceptions include major repairs or structural alterations (up to 12 months; send dates, details of the works and photos).'+EN_BRT,[NF,NF2,NF3,NF4,NF5,EN_BR]),
 dates=EDATES('26 Feb 2024: Full Council approved the second homes premium')),
})
