BCP_LEVY=(EN_LEVY[0]+' In Bournemouth, Christchurch and Poole, larger hotels in a proposed Accommodation Business Improvement District voted for a charge of £2 per room per night (ballot result published by the council on 14 May 2024), but the government declared the ballot void in January 2025 after some eligible businesses did not get ballot papers, so no charge is collected (as reported by the Bournemouth Echo). It would not have applied to typical holiday lets anyway. In December 2025 the council said BCP is not in a strategic mayoral authority area and asked the government to let areas like it use the new overnight visitor levy; in June 2026 the government said it had consulted on this and would respond in due course (as reported by BBC News).',EN_LEVY[1]+[BCAB,BCAE,BCLV,BCBB])
CX.update({
'bournemouth-christchurch-and-poole':dict(lic=(EN_LIC2[0]+' On 12 May 2026 Full Council unanimously backed a motion on short-term lets (minute 111): the council will look at how environmental health, planning enforcement, anti-social behaviour and licensing powers can tackle nuisance from short lets, map short-term lets, promote reporting routes for residents, start preparing for the national register, and ask to join any government pilot on regulating short lets. None of this creates a local licence or registration scheme yet.',EN_LIC2[1]+[BCM]),
 ctrl=('No short-let control area or Article 4 direction for holiday lets (searched 26 Oct 2026). BCP\'s Article 4 directions on houses in multiple occupation (all of the Bournemouth area since December 2011, and Talbot Village in Poole since 1 May 2013) cover changing a house into a small HMO, not holiday lets, and the national planning data register lists none for the council. Whether letting a whole home is a material change of use is decided case by case. The motion Full Council passed on 12 May 2026 asks officers to investigate whether homes let for more than 90 nights a year are a material change of use, to gather evidence that could support an Article 4 direction in future, and to use the new Local Plan to set policies on short-term lets. Nothing has been decided: the new BCP Local Plan is at an early stage (the council\'s timetable shows consultation on a proposed plan from September 2027 and indicative adoption in April 2029). In the Bournemouth area, the Tourist Accommodation Supplementary Planning Document (adopted April 2016 by the former Bournemouth Borough Council) aims to keep existing tourist accommodation and says new self-catering or serviced holiday flats are acceptable where the holiday use is secured by condition or legal agreement. Check with the planning team before converting a home or outbuilding.',[BCH,BCM,BCLP,BCSPD,'https://www.planning.data.gov.uk/entity/?dataset=article-4-direction&organisation_entity=54','https://www.gov.uk/guidance/letting-out-a-self-catering-holiday-home-in-england-rules-and-regulations']),
 levy=BCP_LEVY,
 fees=('No short-let fees. Council tax: Full Council on 9 Jan 2024 (59 votes to 3, with 3 abstentions) approved the formal determination, following its decision on 12 Jul 2022 to charge a second homes premium. The council\'s page says the premium was introduced from 1 Apr 2025, so a furnished home that is nobody\'s main home pays 200% council tax. There is no discount for a furnished home that is not your main home or is between lets, unless it is needed for your job. '+EN_EXC+' BCP\'s operational guidance lists only these national exceptions, with no extra local ones. You must send evidence, for example listings on sales or letting websites with the date marketing started; the marketing exception applies only once per taxpayer, and the council expects to be told of changes within 21 days. In cases of exceptional hardship you can apply for a discretionary reduction (section 13A). Empty and unfurnished homes pay a premium after 1 year (since 1 Apr 2024): the bill is 200% after 1 year, 300% after 5 years and 400% after 10 years.'+EN_BRT+' The Valuation Office Agency, not the council, decides whether a let moves to business rates.',[BC,BC2,BC3,BC4,EN_BR]),
 dates=['12 Jul 2022: Council decided to apply a second homes premium','9 Jan 2024: Full Council approved the formal determination','14 May 2024: Accommodation BID ballot result published (approved)','Jan 2025: Accommodation BID ballot declared void by government (as reported by the Bournemouth Echo)','1 Apr 2025: 100% second homes premium started','12 May 2026: Full Council backed the short-term lets motion unanimously','Mar 2027: England short-term let register expected (GOV.UK)']),
'isles-of-scilly':dict(lic=EN_LIC2,
 ctrl=('No short-let control area or Article 4 direction aimed at holiday-let use (searched 26 Oct 2026). The national planning data register lists four Article 4 directions for the Council of the Isles of Scilly (1975, 1988, 1995 and 1998); they remove permitted development rights for house extensions and alterations, temporary uses of land, agricultural works, swimming pools, temporary buildings, and roof, window, door and painting changes in the Conservation Area, so many works to a holiday let need planning permission even where they would not elsewhere. The whole of the islands is a Conservation Area and a National Landscape (AONB). The Isles of Scilly Local Plan 2015-2030 (adopted 25 Mar 2021) is strict on holiday lets: Policy WC5 says converting a property for tourism must not lose homes that would otherwise be available for permanent occupation unless there are wider public benefits, and an informal bed and breakfast or extra holiday letting accommodation within the curtilage of a home will not be permitted unless a certificate of lawful use as a C1 guesthouse has been obtained; Policy LC8 says a replacement dwelling, extension or alteration must not include any self-contained holiday letting accommodation unless this is already lawful; Policy LC1 says any open-market homes allowed to fund affordable housing will be conditioned as principal residence homes; and Policy LC2 says all new affordable homes must be occupied as the principal residence of someone with a local housing need. The council has used section 106 agreements to restrict who can occupy new homes since at least the 1990s, so check the title of any property for an occupancy restriction. A Local Plan review is under way (timetable approved by Full Council on 26 Feb 2026). Talk to the planning team (planning@scilly.gov.uk) before letting or converting a property.',[SCLP,SCW1,SCL1,SCSLN,SCLDS,SCDP,'https://www.planning.data.gov.uk/entity/?dataset=article-4-direction&organisation_entity=178']),
 levy=EN_LEVY,
 fees=('No short-let fees. Council tax: on 22 Feb 2024 Full Council approved a budget that included bringing in premiums for empty homes and second homes, and the council\'s Empty Homes and Second Homes Premium Policy 2026-27 confirms a 100% premium on second homes from 1 Apr 2025, so a furnished home that is nobody\'s main home pays double council tax. '+EN_EXC+' The council adds a local exception for staff accommodation: no premium where a local employer owns or rents the home for its staff, or where someone employed by an island business rents it and keeps their main home elsewhere in the UK. Second homes of people who must live elsewhere for their job may get a 50% discount. To claim an exception contact revenues@scilly.gov.uk or 0300 1234 105 (option 1) with evidence; hardship linked to a protected characteristic can be considered for a discretionary reduction (section 13A), and appeals go to the Valuation Tribunal. Empty and unfurnished homes pay a 100% premium after 12 months (from 1 Apr 2025), and may get a 50% discount for up to six months first.'+EN_BRT,[SC,SC2,SC3,EN_BR]),
 dates=['25 Mar 2021: Isles of Scilly Local Plan 2015-2030 adopted','22 Feb 2024: Full Council budget approved bringing in empty and second homes premiums','1 Apr 2025: 100% second homes premium started','26 Feb 2026: Local Plan review timetable approved','Mar 2027: England short-term let register expected (GOV.UK)']),
})
import html as _h
def cfaq(c,x):
    nm=c['name'];tx=lambda v:_h.unescape(re.sub(r'<[^>]+>','',v)).strip()
    Q=[(f'Do I need a short-term let licence in {nm}?' if c['nation']=='Scotland' else f'Do I need a licence or registration for a short-term let in {nm}?',x['lic'][0]),(f'Do I need planning permission for a short-term let in {nm}?',x['ctrl'][0]),(f'Is there a visitor levy (tourist tax) in {nm}?',x['levy'][0]),(f'How much does a short-term let licence cost in {nm}?' if c['nation']=='Scotland' else f'What fees apply to short-term lets in {nm}?',x['fees'][0])]
    Q=[(q,tx(a)) for q,a in Q if a and tx(a)]
    ld=json.dumps({'@context':'https://schema.org','@type':'FAQPage','mainEntity':[{'@type':'Question','name':q,'acceptedAnswer':{'@type':'Answer','text':a}} for q,a in Q]}).replace('</','<\\/')
    h=f'<h2>{e(nm)} short-let rules: quick answers</h2>'+''.join(f'<details><summary>{e(q)}</summary><p>{e(a)}</p></details>' for q,a in Q)+'<p class="small">Answers summarise the sections above. Follow each section\'s official source before acting.</p>'
    return h,f'<script type="application/ld+json">{ld}</script>'
ICO={'lic':'\U0001F4CB','ctrl':'\U0001F3D8','levy':'\U0001F4B7','fees':'\U0001F4B0'}
def sec(h,x,k=''):
    st='' if k=='fees' else '<span class="st yes">Registration required</span>' if 'must register' in x[0] else ('<span class="st no">None found</span>' if x[0].strip().startswith('No ') else '<span class="st yes">Applies</span>')
    return f'<section class="rc" aria-labelledby="rc-{k}"><div class="rch"><span class="ri" aria-hidden="true">{ICO.get(k,"")}</span><h2 id="rc-{k}">{h}</h2>{st}</div><p>{x[0]}</p>'+(f'<p class="small rs">Source: {src(x[1])}</p>' if x[1] else '')+'</section>'
def CK(c):
    """Latest date this council's rules are backed as checked: the last full sweep (data.json 'checked'),
    or a later changes-log entry naming the council (e.g. its page being added from official sources)."""
    k=c['name'].split(' (')[0].replace('City of ','').replace(' City','')
    rx=re.compile(r'(?<!North )(?<!South )(?<!East )(?<!West )\b'+re.escape(k)+r'\b')
    return max([C]+[ch['date'] for ch in d['changes'] if rx.search(ch['text'])])
def WHAT(c):
    import datetime as _d
    m=[ch for ch in d['changes'] if c['name'].split(' (')[0].replace('City of ','').replace(' City','') in ch['text'] and not ch['text'].startswith('Added')]
    if m:
        ch=max(m,key=lambda z:z['date']);dd=_d.date.fromisoformat(ch['date']).strftime('%-d %b %Y')
        return f'<p class="what"><b>What changed ({dd}):</b> {e(ch["text"])} <a href="{e(ch["source"])}" rel="nofollow">Source</a> · <a href="#get-alerts">Get free alerts</a></p>'
    return f'<p class="what"><b>Last checked {_d.date.fromisoformat(CK(c)).strftime("%-d %b %Y")}:</b> no change logged for this council in our <a href="../changes.html">changes log</a>. <a href="#get-alerts">Get free alerts</a> when it changes.</p>'
